  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 304 AM EDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nThe mid-level ridge will begin to break down at the beginning of \nthe long-term period on Thursday as a shortwave trough drops \nsoutheastward. This will bring a surface cold front southward into\nthe Ohio Valley, serving to increase PoPs by late week as \nmoisture interacts with the weak frontal boundary. This front now \nlooks to become stationary farther to our north than previously \nfavored, likely across the region from the Ohio Valley to \nTennessee Valley Friday into the coming weekend. With deeper \nmoisture pooling in advance of the front, expect elevated \ndiurnally-enhanced rain chances through the weekend, and the \npotential for locally heavy rainfall may arise.\n\nAny cooler and drier conditions will remain locked well to our \nnorth through the coming weekend. Highs will remain well above \nnormal in the 90s through the week for most outside of the \nmountains. With dewpoints remaining near or above 70, heat index \nvalues in the upper 90s to low 100s will, things will continue \nfeeling decidedly summery for at least another week.",
